>
  <div class="article-callout">
    <p><strong>Archive note:</strong> This launch post is retained for project history. Product details and availability have changed since it was first published.</p>
  </div>

  <p>
    It is here. After almost two years of work, Authier reached the point where we
    could run the service in production and invite people outside the team to try
    it.
  </p>

  <p>
    The idea started with a frustration: secure login tools often make the common
    path feel harder than it needs to be. We wanted the transparency of an
    open-source password manager, the convenience of strong browser autofill, and
    a clearer role for devices in account security.
  </p>

  <h2>Built by two people, in public</h2>

  <p>
    I brought the original idea, but Authier would not have reached a usable state
    without Petr. Turning a security-sensitive prototype into an extension,
    mobile client, backend, and deployment pipeline required far more than a good
    concept.
  </p>

  <p>
    Publishing the source was not a launch flourish. It was part of the premise:
    users and researchers should be able to inspect how credentials are encrypted,
    how a device is approved, and how the service changes over time.
  </p>

  <h2>Launch was the beginning</h2>

  <p>
    Authier remains a young project. It does not have the audit history or product
    breadth of established password managers. The work after launch has focused on
    modernizing the backend, rebuilding the web vault, improving device flows, and
    keeping browser extensions current.
  </p>

  <p>
    If that direction interests you, the best place to follow it is the
    <a href="https://github.com/authier-pm/authier">public GitHub repository</a>.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>
